# Worktrees are kept in-repo at .worktrees/<branch>, and that directory is ignored through the
# global git ignore rather than this repo's .gitignore. Git honours the global file and Prettier
# does not, so `prettier --check .` walks into every worktree and checks another branch's working
# tree as though it were this one's. Nothing else in `validate` needs this: tsc skips directories
# whose name begins with a dot, and lint and the test run are both scoped to src/.
#
# It goes wrong at the worst moment. An unresolved conflict marker is a syntax error, so a merge
# paused in one worktree fails `validate` in all of them, naming a file on a branch you are not on.
.worktrees
*.d.ts
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json
yarn.lock
